        "text": "This paper proposed a channel-attention-based parameter embedding (CAPE) module which allows any data-driven SciML models to incorporate PDE parameters. We also propose a simple but effective curriculum training strategy that allows us to bridge teacher-forcing and auto-regressive learning. We performed an extensive set of experiments and showed the effectiveness and efficiency of our method from various aspects: generalization of seen/unseen PDE parameters during training, parameter efficiency, and inference time.\nOne of our key findings is the behavior of ML models without parameter embedding. We found that the ML models without parameter embeddings either (1) show a poor performance uniformly for all the PDE parameters (1D Advection eq.), (2) overfitting to a preferred parameter regime (1D Burgers eq. and 2D NS eqs.). Hence, the ML model in this case has no generalization power in terms of PDE parameters. On the other hand, the models with CAPE module generalized well for 1D Burgers\u2019 eq. and 2D NS eqs. whose parameters govern the physical systems\u2019 diffusion behavior. CAPE cannot be generalized for 1D Advection eq., and we consider it necessary to formulate CAPE to be more physics-informed manner which we consider for future work."
